          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %strong
              standard
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28in+particle+physics%29"} model
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle+physics"} particle physics
            is a
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28in+particle+physics%29"} model (in particle physics)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            that describes the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+particles"} fundamental particles
            currently experimentally known, containing
          %ul
            %li
              %strong>
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matter"} matter
              \:
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electrons"} electrons
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neutrinos"} neutrinos
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quarks"} quarks
              and other
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermions"} fermions
          %p
            as well as three of the four
            %em
              fundamental
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forces"} forces
            as currently known, which, somewhat roughly, are
          %ul
            %li
              %strong>
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/force"} force
              \: the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electroweak+field"} electroweak force
              (including
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetism"} electromagnetism
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+nuclear+force"} strong nuclear force
          %p
            It is defined as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+Lagrangian"} local Lagrangian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theory
            which is an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Maxwell-Yang-Mills-Dirac-Higgs+theory"} Einstein-Maxwell-Yang-Mills-Dirac-Higgs theory
          %p
            The main ingredient missing from the standard model is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum
            version of the field of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
          %p
            As a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang–Mills
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinors+in+Yang-Mills+theory"} spinors in Yang–Mills theory
          %p
            Although there are several approaches to formulate a mathematically precise definition of what a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            is, there is no rigorous formulation (yet) that comprises the whole standard model.
