          %h2#TensionWithExperiment Tension with experiment and Incompleteness
          %p
            The standard model of particle physics is supported by a tremendous level of confirmation by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LHC"} LHC
            experiment. But there are indictations of discrepancies, pointing to “new physics”. See at
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flavour+anomaly"} flavour anomaly
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anomalous+magnetic+moment"}> anomalous magnetic moment
                –
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "anomalous+magnetic+moment#Anomalies"} anomalies
          %p Not quite in tension with the standard model, but still possibly pointing to unaccounted effects is the
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+field"}> Higgs field
              \-
              %a{:href => "Higgs+field#MassAndVacuumInstability"}> metastability
              \.
          %p
            Then there are experimental phenomena which are known to be implied by the standard model by way of computer simulation (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice+QCD"}> lattice QCD
            ), but which are not conceptually understood:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confinement
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass+gap+problem"}> mass gap problem
              )
          %p
            Finally there is the evident and notorious fact that the standard model simply does not include any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            of the fourth known fundamental
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/force"} force
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
          %p for more on this.
          %p
            %br/
          %h2#variations_and_generalizations Variations and generalizations
          %p
            There is a plethora of attempts and suggestions for variations and generalizations of the standard model into models that may resolve the tension with experiment mentioned
            %a{:href => "#TensionWithExperiment"} above
            and which may be conceptually more satisfying from the point of view of models in theoretical physics.
          %h3#kaluzaklein_theory Kaluza–Klein theory
          %p
            Shortly after the conception of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} general relativity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            alone may effectively appear – if considered on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            that is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle"} bundle
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber"} fiber
            has a tiny volume (as meaured by the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+metric"} Riemannian metric
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+mechanism"} Kaluza-Klein mechanism
          %p
            The huge conceptual simplification that this observation suggested had excited theoreticians early on, but a problem of Kaluza–Klein models is that not only does the “compactified” theory of gravity as if by magic emulate
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            %em not
            experimentally observed.
          %p
            For that reason interest in Kaluza–Klein theories had decreased in the middle of the last century. Physics departments saw a major revival of the idea when
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            (see below) gained interest, since that theory necessarily exhibits a Kaluza–Klein mechanism. Incidentally, the problem of the spurious fields – the moduli – was still present in this approach. For more on this see the entry
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/landscape+of+string+theory+vacua"} landscape of string theory vacua
          %h3#guts GUTs
          %p
            One of the oldest studies of variations of the standard model is the investigation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GUT"} grand unified theories
            (GUTs), which are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang–Mills theories
            that instead of the standard model gauge group have a bigger gauge group which is however a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+group"} simple group
          %h3#NCGeometry Noncommutative geometry
          %p
            A widespread perception is that some of the conceptual problems with the standard model point to the fact that some basic assumption of 20th century physics on the nature of reality is oversimplified. According to the approach of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} noncommutative geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            as a smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            is an oversimplification that makes itself felt when the quantization of the force of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            becomes relevant.
          %p
            In a class of “noncommutative” generalizations of the standard model, spacetime is therefore replaced more generally by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+triple"} spectral triple
            that models a possibly “noncommutative space”. One of the more successful approaches in this direction is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Connes-Lott-Chamseddine+model"} Connes-Lott-Chamseddine model
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+theory"} Kaluza-Klein theory
            (see above), but with the crucial difference that the fiber in the KK-picture is a highly non-classical non-commutative space, whose classical dimension is that of a point, but whose intrinsic dimension is 6. (This is incidentally the same value of the internal dimension as suggest by
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
          %p For more on this see
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
              ,
              %em Connes on Spectral Geometry of the Standard Model
              (
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2006/09/connes_on_spectral_geometry_of.html"}> part I
              ,
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2006/09/connes_on_spectral_geometry_of_1.html"} part II
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2006/09/connes_on_spectral_geometry_of_2.html"} part III
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2006/09/connes_on_spectral_geometry_of_3.html"} part IV
          %h3#string_theory String theory
          %p
            A more drastic theoretical modifications to the standard model is proposed in the context of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            is proposed to be refined by something else. As opposed to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GUTs"} GUTs
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            could be incorporated into the picture.
          %p See
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersecting+D-brane+models"} intersecting D-brane models
            %li
              %p
